{
 "type": "FeatureCollection",
 "metadata": {
  "note": "The Illinois Central's Birmingham District — the line that carried the IC's Florida trains off the Chicago–New Orleans main line at Fulton, Ky. and south through Jackson, Tenn., Corinth, Miss. and Haleyville, Ala. to Birmingham, where the Central of Georgia took them on. The IC went looking for a Birmingham route in 1901, chose the country between Jackson and Jasper, and had a 216-mile Jackson–Birmingham corridor working by April 1908, built from new construction, purchase and trackage rights. Added 2026-08-27. Drawn 1908–1971, ending with the City of Miami's last run the day before Amtrak. The Chicago–Fulton half of the Seminole's and the City of Miami's journey is the main line already drawn in illinois_central.geojson and is not repeated here.",
  "not_yet_sourced": "Every headway_min here is an estimate. The IC's own rails ended at Haleyville and its trains ran on the Southern Railway to Jasper and the Frisco into Birmingham, but the exact junction points of those trackage rights have not been established, so the Haleyville–Birmingham geometry is the modern route rather than a verified 1908 one. Whether any local service ran over the district between the 1908 completion and the Seminole's 1909 debut has not been checked.",
  "source": "Wikipedia (Illinois Central Railroad, Seminole, City of Miami), american-rails.com, Trains Magazine",
  "pulled": "20260827"
 },
