    "avg_speed_kmh": 65,
    "speed_source": "Estimated, not sourced. The one schedule found — the Seminole from 1950, Chicago 4 pm to Birmingham 8.30 am — covers the Chicago–Birmingham journey as a whole, not this district on its own, so it cannot be turned into a speed for these miles.",
    "geometry_quality": "Real-traced from OpenStreetMap, with no straight-line approximation anywhere on the route. The Fulton-to-Jackson third is OSM geometry used verbatim: the ways named 'West Tennessee Railroad' that still carry old_railway_operator=IC chain into an unbroken 63.7-mile line. The rest -- Jackson south through Henderson and Selmer to Corinth, Miss., on through Fulton, Miss. to Haleyville, Ala., and over the Southern and the Frisco to Jasper and Birmingham -- was routed leg by leg as a corridor-weighted shortest path over the railway ways near each documented town, and every one of the 11 legs came back on real mapped track: none had to be drawn as a chord. Traced length 282.3 miles, against a documented 216 miles Jackson-Birmingham plus roughly 64 miles Fulton-Jackson -- about 280, which is the check that the routing followed the right corridor rather than wandering onto a neighbouring railroad. CAVEAT: south of Haleyville the Illinois Central owned no track. Its trains ran on the Southern Railway to Jasper and the Frisco into Birmingham, so that stretch is drawn on the modern successor route and is trackage the IC used under rights rather than track it owned. CAVEAT (CLAUDE.md Rule 2): modern alignment drawn back to 1908, not checked against a period valuation map.",
    "geometry_source": [
     "OpenStreetMap ways tagged old_railway_operator=IC (West Tennessee Railroad, Memphis District IC District, Redmont Railway) plus the Southern/Frisco successor route south of Haleyville, pulled via Overpass 2026-08-27"
    ],
    "segments": [
     {
      "start": 1908,
      "end": 1909,
      "headway_min": 1440,
      "note": "The Illinois Central began scouting a route to Birmingham in 1901 and settled on the country between Jackson, Tenn. and Jasper, Ala. By April 1908, through a combination of new construction, purchase and trackage rights, it had a 216-mile corridor from Jackson to Birmingham. Its own rails reached Haleyville, Ala.; beyond that its trains used the Southern Railway to Jasper and the Frisco into Birmingham. At the north end the district joined the Chicago–New Orleans main line at Fulton, Ky. headway_min is an estimate, not read off a timetable.",
      "sources": [
       "Segment-level: the 1901 survey, the April 1908 completion of the 216-mile Jackson–Birmingham corridor and the Haleyville–Jasper–Birmingham trackage rights over the Southern and the Frisco are carried by the references below; the headway is NOT sourced.",
